Read all five documents. No source code, ADRs, or collab logs consulted, and I did not reopen my own draft. Findings below, most severe first. Line numbers refer to `docs/design/dao.lease.md` unless stated.

## High

**H1 | lines 275, 290, 188-191 | "emphatically not a timer"; "the clock is read there, never armed"; judgment points are "points the grantor's composition already passes through."**
The JIT's safe points are reached by running. A judge's points are reached by being driven, and silence is exactly what stops driving an event-driven interpreter. On JS and Dart nothing runs without an event, so a grantor whose only holder has gone quiet passes through no point and never judges, with its process alive. The promise at line 278 then fails on two of three hosts. The governing document already allows the fix: `datom.world.md` line 66 lists "a timer fires" among host events an adapter deposits as plain data. Correction: replace "emphatically not a timer" with "never a timer that holds a callback; a tick deposited as data by a host adapter and read by the judge is the ordinary form of a declared cadence." Say that the judge is an interpreter over two streams, renewals and ticks, and drop the claim that its cadence comes free.

**H2 | lines 169-173 | authorship is "the ambient capability this architecture already has" because "streams are single-writer and boundary events are attributed."**
`dao.stream.md` grants neither. Its Concurrency section makes one-log-per-writer an interpretation a composition may choose, and its descriptor section makes `:dao.stream/attachment` optional. Attribution exists on WebSocket boundaries and nowhere else. Two of the three justifying uses, the forwarder pause and the shared-work claim, run over in-process media where any writer can append an `:accepted` fact, so "only grantor-authored facts establish terms" is unenforceable there. The document half-admits this at line 372, buried in the delegation section. Correction: move that admission to the "Renewal is evidence" section and state the precondition as a rule. The negotiation half is trustworthy only on a medium where the judge can establish authorship, by single-writer streams per party or by attributed boundary events. On any other medium the facts are unauthenticated data and the composition's own trust is the only guard.

**H3 | lines 384-390 | `dao.stream.ws.md` "owns only that: ... a reclaimed connection closes with a distinguishing close code."**
The WebSocket document defines one distinguishing close code, for a served stream that ended, and a client that reads it stops and does not reattach. A reclaimed connection is not an ended stream. Either reclaim reuses that code, and the client wrongly stops, or reclaim needs a second code that no document defines. Meanwhile the WebSocket document's Deferred section lists liveness probing and "how many unanswered probes end a connection" as open. That is the lease's canonical case, so either the lease document resolves it, and the transport document changes, or the canonical case has no carriage. Both directions of "touches nothing" fail here. Correction: say that a reclaimed connection surfaces to the client as an ordinary `:ws/closed` and that reattaching yields a fresh unsolicited grant. If a distinct code is wanted, list it as an item the transport document must add.

**H4 | lines 103-110, 399-402 | the vocabulary names lease identity, status, duration, max, and cause; nothing names what is leased.**
No key names the resource. For unsolicited connection grants the grantor knows the mapping privately. For "a claim an agent leaves on shared work" the proposal must say what it claims, the grant does not repeat it, and the proposal "creates no state." A reader of the `:accepted` fact alone cannot tell what was granted, which contradicts line 12's "a lease's whole state is readable data." The same use case exposes a second omission: the holder has no stop rule. Renewals are never acknowledged, so a holder cannot know its renewals were observed, and the document never says a holder must cease acting once its own clock exceeds the duration since its last renewal. A paused agent that resumes after lapse acts on work the grantor has reclaimed and regranted. Correction: add a subject key, or state that the grant must carry it, and add a holder-side rule. A holder treats the duration as a bound on its own action, not only on its renewal cadence, and this vocabulary provides no exclusion beyond that bound.

**H5 | lines 235-237 vs 249-254, 255-256 | judge state is "derived from the stream, rebuildable from it"; after restart "a judge knows nothing about silence."**
Both cannot hold. Renewals carry no time, so observation times exist only on the judge's clock and are not rebuildable. Worse, the grant itself is a stream element under ordinary retention, so on a ring buffer the `:accepted` fact can be evicted while the lease is live. A judge restarting then cannot read the duration or the cap and cannot judge at all. The restart rule at line 254 assumes the terms survive. Correction: state that only the identity of the newest renewal is rebuildable, and add the clean restart rule: a grantor that restarts re-grants unsolicited for every resource it still possesses, and the earlier lease is over by that act.

## Medium

**M1 | lines 269-273 | an ungoverned grantor is "refused when the composition is built, exactly as `dao.stream.ws.md` refuses a reject-mode deposit destination."**
The transport constructor can inspect a destination's declared retention. Nothing can inspect whether a composition will append a map with `:dao.lease/status :accepted`. There is no assembly-time hook, and inventing one means a declared grantor property or component, which the document says it adds none of. Correction: either drop the assembly analogy and state the duty as documentation only, or admit the declaration and own it.

**M2 | lines 135-137, 183-186 | "judging is interpretation, reclaiming is execution"; `:lapsed` is appended "when the grantor acts", as "the record of a judgment applied."**
No fact stands between judgment and act. The possessor's interpreter judges, closes the socket, then records. That is the same interpreter doing both, which the document calls the invariant against collapsing interpretation and execution while doing it. The precedent does the same: the VM evaluates its guard, deopts, and records. Correction: stop claiming the invariant for this split. Claim only what holds, that anyone may judge and only the possessor's judgment has effect. Also settle the order: act first and the record may never land, record first and the act may fail. Say which, and what a failed `append!` of `:lapsed` means.

**M3 | lines 136, 141-143 | "`:lapsed` is the deopt datom" and "there is no `:rolled-back`, deliberately."**
In the precedent the VM's deopt falls back to baseline, and its past execution stands. That is exactly what line 142 says a lapse is. The precedent's `:rolled-back` is the status that most plausibly records a deopt, so `:lapsed` is `:rolled-back` renamed, and line 100's "where a status means the same, it is the same word" is violated by the document's own rule. Correction: either adopt `:rolled-back` or say plainly that the word is renamed because its connotation is wrong. Do not present a rename as an omission.

**M4 | lines 27-31 | the precedent mapping has three divergences.**
There is a fourth, and it causes the first two. In the precedent the party that emits the trace is the party that applies patches, the VM. Here the party that emits renewals is the holder, and the applier is the grantor. A patch guard is stateless because the VM predicates over its own values at its own safe point. A lease guard is stateful and needs an observed window because the guard's subject is remote and its evidence crosses a medium. Correction: name "the guard's subject is not the applier" as the divergence, and present the first two as its consequences. The three named ones are not spurious.

**M5 | lines 119-123 | "a proposal creates no state" resolves the lifecycle split.**
It resolves the grantor's side and leaves the holder's. The document's thesis is that silence is evidence only over a declared window, yet a proposal has no duration, so the holder judges silence with no discipline at all. Nothing obliges a grantor to answer, `:rejected` is given no purpose that silence does not already serve, and line 133 has the grantor "grant less than was asked," which means it did read the proposal. The rule is a paper-over. Correction: keep `:proposed` and `:accepted`, since the shared-work use needs a holder-initiated lease. Add three sentences: a grantor owes no answer; `:rejected` is courtesy exactly as `:released` is; a holder's wait on a proposal is bounded by nothing in this vocabulary, and it is an ordinary unanswered request. Also state that after `:rejected` the identity is spent.

**M6 | lines 246-254 | "a gap is not silence"; unknown after "a gap spanning the watched window."**
"Watched window" is undefined, and the rule is looser than the facts allow. After any gap, a judge cannot distinguish "no renewals during the gap" from "renewals during the gap, since evicted." The retained tail after the gap either contains a renewal for a lease or it does not. Correction: after any gap, a lease with no renewal in the retained tail is unknown, and the one-duration rule applies. Also, line 247 asserts oldest-first eviction for all media, and `dao.stream.md` standardizes no retention policy. Qualify it as the medium's declared nature.

**M7 | lines 275-280 | the obligation "is the same obligation a reader's runtime already carries when it retries `:dao.stream/blocked`"; the promise is reclaim "attempts ... until the resource is reclaimed or authoritatively found absent."**
`dao.stream.md` says a reader retries "when it chooses" and that no runtime is required, so no such obligation exists to borrow. The promise guarantees attempts, not reclaim, and "authoritatively found absent" has no meaning for a resource the grantor itself possesses. "Attempts" also conflicts with at most one `:lapsed` fact per lease. Correction: state the promise as one the grantor makes about itself. A grantor that judges at a declared cadence reclaims every lapsed lease within duration plus cadence plus the reclaim's own cost. Say where the cadence is declared, because no document currently gives it a home.

## Low

- **L1 | line 129 | `:dao.lease/duration`.** No unit. A grant crosses a wire, so the unit is interoperable content. State it.
- **L2 | lines 162, 313 | "renegotiation is a new proposal"; past the cap a holder "proposes anew."** With "at most one fact per status" the new proposal needs a new identity, and nothing says whether the old lease continues, is superseded, or must be released. Say that a resource may carry several leases and that supersession is the grantor's `:lapsed` with cause.
- **L3 | lines 215-219 | "delay errs safe."** Safe for the holder only. Judge lag adds directly to tenure, and the document's own stated failure mode at line 56 is the leak. Say that delay errs toward the holder and that the leak is bounded by the judge's declared lag.
- **L4 | line 301 | "no DaoStream operation may consult a lease."** A subordinate document legislating over its superior. It is already implied by the no-hidden-state invariant. Rephrase as an observation.
- **L5 | lines 111-112 | identity "unique among that party's leases."** Two holders on one medium may mint the same identity, so the judge must key by author and identity, which returns to H2.
- **L6 | throughout | grantor, judge, possessor.** Three names for one party, except at line 185 where "anyone" may judge. Define the terms once.

## What an implementer must invent

Which stream each of the five facts lands on for the connection case, given the client cannot read after the socket closes. How a holder learns the identity and duration of an unsolicited grant. Whether the lease identity is the attachment identity. The judge's cadence, tolerance, and where either is declared. The order of act and record, and the response to a failed `:lapsed` append. The reclaim retry policy. The duration unit. The renewal fraction. The holder's stop rule. What names the leased resource. Whether a resource admits one lease or many, and how contention between proposals is decided, which is not the deferred authorization question.

## The thing most likely wrong that I cannot prove wrong

The predicate itself. The document says renewal is "evidence the holder is alive" because it comes "from its own control flow." On an event-loop host the loop keeps turning while the work it was doing is wedged, so a holder that renews from its loop renews honestly by the letter and falsely in fact. The lease then proves that the renewer's loop is alive, not that the holder is making progress, which is the false liveness the document rejects Jini for. For a served connection that is the right predicate, because the connection is the work. For a claim on shared work I suspect it is the wrong one, and the vocabulary has no place for progress evidence. I cannot prove it because a holder that renews only from inside its work step satisfies the document, and the document does not distinguish that holder from the other.
